export const DATA_64: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-088", num: 88, title: "Đề 088", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Why Some People Work Better in the Afternoon", paragraphs: ["Have you ever noticed that some people jump out of bed full of energy in the morning, while others feel truly awake only after midday? This difference is not simply a matter of habit or laziness. Scientists have discovered that each person has a natural internal clock, known as a chronotype, which controls when they feel most alert, when they prefer to eat, and when their body is ready for sleep. Understanding your chronotype can help you plan your day more effectively and get more out of your working hours.", "Most people fall into one of two broad categories: morning types, sometimes called \"larks,\" and evening types, known as \"owls.\" Morning types wake up naturally at an early hour, feel their sharpest in the first half of the day, and tend to become tired by early evening. Evening types, on the other hand, take longer to feel fully awake after rising and reach their peak mental performance in the afternoon or even late at night. Research suggests that roughly 25 percent of the population are strong morning types, another 25 percent are strong evening types, and the remaining half fall somewhere in between.", "The reason for these differences lies in biology. Inside the human brain is a region called the suprachiasmatic nucleus, which acts as the body's master clock. This tiny cluster of nerve cells responds to changes in light and darkness and sends signals to the rest of the body to adjust hormone levels, body temperature, and alertness. In morning types, the cycle of these biological signals peaks earlier in the day. In evening types, the same cycle is shifted later, meaning that hormones associated with wakefulness and concentration reach their highest levels in the afternoon rather than the morning.", "For people with an evening chronotype, trying to perform demanding mental tasks early in the morning can feel genuinely difficult — not because they are less capable, but because their brain chemistry is simply not yet in its optimal state. Studies have shown that when evening types are tested on memory, problem-solving, and concentration tasks in the morning, they often score significantly lower than morning types. However, when the same tests are conducted in the afternoon, the gap between the two groups narrows considerably, and evening types sometimes perform at a higher level.", "The mismatch between a person's natural chronotype and the demands of a standard working or school day causes what scientists call \"social jetlag.\" This term describes the feeling of tiredness and poor concentration that results from regularly waking up and working at a time that does not suit the body's natural rhythm. Social jetlag is particularly common among teenagers and young adults, whose biological clocks naturally shift toward evening preferences during adolescence. Many education researchers argue that later school start times would lead to improved attention, better academic results, and fewer behavioral problems among students.", "Of course, not every workplace or school can simply change its hours to match each person's ideal schedule. However, there are practical steps individuals can take to make the most of their chronotype. Evening types, for instance, can reserve their most important and creative work for the afternoon, when they are at their mental best, and use the morning for routine tasks that require less focus. Spending time outdoors in natural light early in the day can also help shift the body clock slightly earlier over time, reducing the severity of social jetlag without completely changing a person's natural rhythms."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Morning people are generally more productive and successful than evening people.", "People have a natural internal clock that affects when they work best, and understanding it can improve daily performance.", "Teenagers should be allowed to sleep later because they are naturally lazier than adults.", "The human brain works in exactly the same way for everyone, regardless of lifestyle."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what proportion of the population are strong evening types?", options: ["About half of all people", "Roughly a quarter", "Fewer than one in twenty", "Around one in ten"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the role of the suprachiasmatic nucleus?", options: ["It produces hormones that regulate hunger and determine when a person feels like eating.", "It controls breathing rate and heart rhythm during periods of physical exercise.", "It stores memories formed during sleep and transfers them to long-term storage.", "It responds to light and dark signals and coordinates the body's daily biological rhythms."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happens when evening types and morning types are given the same tests in the afternoon?", options: ["The difference in scores between the two groups becomes much smaller.", "Evening types consistently score lower than they do in morning sessions.", "Morning types continue to outperform evening types by a wide margin.", "Both groups show a significant drop in performance compared to morning results."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the term \"social jetlag\" mean?", options: ["The frustration people feel when their colleagues have different working styles", "The fatigue and poor focus that result from working at times that do not match one's natural body clock", "The tiredness caused by traveling across multiple time zones for work", "The difficulty teenagers experience when adjusting to a new school timetable"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the biological signals the body uses to regulate hormones, temperature, and alertness", "the nerve cells located inside the suprachiasmatic nucleus", "the changes in natural light and darkness that occur throughout the day", "the differences in sleep preferences between morning and evening types"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as practical advice for evening types?", options: ["Going to bed earlier each night to build a habit of waking up before sunrise", "Getting exposure to natural light early in the day to gradually shift the body clock", "Using the morning hours for tasks that require less concentration", "Saving complex or creative tasks for the afternoon when the mind is sharper"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"optimal\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["its average or typical level of activity", "its most comfortable and relaxed condition", "its slowest and least responsive phase", "its best or most effective state"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"mismatch\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["an argument between people who disagree about working schedules", "a general feeling of boredom caused by doing the same task repeatedly", "a situation in which two things do not fit or work well together", "a gradual improvement that happens when two systems are brought into balance"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the current school system and teenagers' chronotypes?", options: ["Teenagers with morning chronotypes are better suited to academic life than those with evening chronotypes.", "Most schools already offer flexible start times to accommodate students with different chronotypes.", "Social jetlag only affects teenagers who sleep fewer than six hours per night.", "Many teenagers are required to study and concentrate at times when their biological clocks are not yet at their peak."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Modern Problem of Sleep Deprivation", paragraphs: ["Sleep was once treated by medical science as a largely passive state — a kind of biological standby mode in which the body rested while the mind temporarily switched off. Decades of neuroscientific research have dismantled this view entirely. Sleep is now understood to be one of the most metabolically active and functionally critical periods of the human biological day, during which the brain consolidates memories, clears toxic waste products, regulates emotional responses, and carries out essential repairs to virtually every major organ system. Given this understanding, the scale of sleep deprivation in modern industrialized societies constitutes what the World Health Organization has described as a global public health crisis.", "The statistics are striking . In the United States, surveys consistently show that more than one-third of adults sleep fewer than the seven hours per night recommended by the American Academy of Sleep Medicine. In Japan, South Korea, and the United Kingdom, comparable data reveal similarly high proportions of chronically under-slept populations. Among adolescents, the figures are particularly alarming: a study of secondary school students across twelve countries found that fewer than 30 percent were obtaining the eight to ten hours of nightly sleep that researchers consider appropriate for their developmental stage. The consequences of this widespread shortfall extend far beyond daytime drowsiness.", "The physiological effects of chronic sleep deprivation are both extensive and well-documented. Sustained sleep loss has been associated with significantly elevated risks of cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, and obesity — conditions linked to the disruption of hormones that regulate appetite, blood sugar, and inflammatory responses. The immune system is also profoundly affected: studies have demonstrated that individuals who sleep fewer than six hours per night are more than four times as likely to develop a cold when exposed to a rhinovirus compared with those sleeping seven hours or more. Perhaps most concerning is the relationship between poor sleep and mental health. Chronic sleep deprivation is both a symptom and a contributing cause of depression, anxiety, and a range of other psychiatric conditions, creating a feedback loop that is difficult to interrupt without targeted intervention.", "Cognitive performance suffers acutely under sleep deprivation. Research published in prominent peer-reviewed journals has shown that after seventeen hours without sleep, an individual's performance on tasks measuring reaction time, decision-making, and sustained attention is equivalent to that of someone with a blood alcohol level of 0.05 percent — a level considered legally impaired for driving in many countries. After twenty-four hours of wakefulness, the impairment corresponds to a blood alcohol level of 0.10 percent, which exceeds legal driving limits in virtually all jurisdictions. Despite this , surveys indicate that large numbers of people regularly drive, operate machinery, or make high-stakes professional decisions while severely sleep-deprived, often unaware of the true extent of their impairment.", "The causes of modern sleep deprivation are multiple and mutually reinforcing. Artificial light — from streetlamps, television screens, and the ubiquitous blue-light emissions of smartphones and tablets — suppresses the production of melatonin, the hormone that signals to the brain that night has arrived and sleep should begin. The economic and cultural value placed on productivity in many societies actively discourages adequate sleep, with long working hours, early school start times, and the glorification of busyness all contributing to a cultural environment in which sleeping less is frequently associated with ambition and dedication rather than recognized as a health risk. Shift work, which affects a substantial proportion of the global workforce in sectors including healthcare, transportation, and manufacturing, imposes further disruption by requiring workers to sleep at times that conflict with the body's natural circadian rhythm.", "Interventions to address sleep deprivation range from the individual to the systemic. At the personal level, sleep hygiene practices — maintaining a consistent sleep schedule, reducing screen use in the hour before bed, keeping the sleeping environment cool and dark, and avoiding caffeine in the afternoon — have been shown to produce modest but meaningful improvements in sleep quality and duration. At the institutional level, a growing number of school districts in the United States and elsewhere have shifted secondary school start times later in the morning, citing research showing improvements in attendance, academic performance, and mental health among students. Some employers have begun introducing workplace policies that recognize adequate sleep as a component of occupational health, discouraging the cultural norm of sleep sacrifice as a marker of professional commitment.", "The scientific consensus on the importance of sleep could hardly be clearer, yet translating that consensus into behavioral and policy change has proven far more difficult than the evidence might suggest. The challenge is partly one of perception: sleep loss, unlike alcohol intoxication, does not produce obvious signs of impairment that others can easily observe, and the cumulative effects of chronic under-sleeping are experienced so gradually that many people adapt to a degraded baseline without recognizing how far their functioning has declined. Closing the gap between what science knows about sleep and how society values it remains one of the more urgent — and underappreciated — public health tasks of our time."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To examine the scope, causes, and consequences of widespread sleep deprivation and discuss possible responses", "To explain why the human brain requires more sleep during periods of stress and emotional difficulty", "To argue that governments should make sleep medicine a compulsory part of school education", "To compare the sleeping habits of people in different countries around the world"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does sleep deprivation affect a person's chances of getting a cold?", options: ["Sleep-deprived people are more than four times as likely to become infected when exposed to a cold virus.", "Under-slept individuals recover from colds more slowly but are no more likely to catch them.", "Sleeping fewer than eight hours reduces the immune system's ability to produce antibodies by half.", "People who sleep fewer than six hours are twice as likely to catch a cold as those who sleep more."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does seventeen hours without sleep do to a person's ability to perform tasks?", options: ["It produces measurable effects only on creative tasks, leaving routine tasks largely unaffected.", "It impairs performance to the same degree as a blood alcohol level of 0.10 percent.", "It reduces performance to a level similar to that of someone who is legally drunk in most countries.", "It causes the brain to enter a microsleep state that lasts for several seconds at unpredictable intervals."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does artificial light contribute to sleep deprivation?", options: ["It suppresses melatonin production, delaying the brain's signal that it is time to sleep.", "It raises body temperature at night, making it physically uncomfortable to fall asleep.", "It increases cortisol levels throughout the night, preventing the body from entering deep sleep.", "It interferes with the brain's ability to store memories formed during the day."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a sleep hygiene practice that can improve sleep quality?", options: ["Limiting total screen time to fewer than two hours per day at any time of day", "Taking a short nap of twenty minutes in the early afternoon to reduce accumulated tiredness", "Keeping the sleeping room at a cool temperature and free from bright light", "Drinking a warm beverage such as herbal tea immediately before getting into bed"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cause of modern sleep deprivation?", options: ["The widespread consumption of caffeinated drinks throughout the working day", "Cultural attitudes that associate sleeping less with hard work and ambition", "The blue light emitted by smartphones and tablets before bedtime", "Shift work schedules that require employees to sleep at biologically inconvenient times"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"this\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the fact that sleep-deprived individuals consistently underestimate their own impairment", "the legal blood alcohol limit for driving, which varies between countries", "the finding that reaction time is the cognitive skill most affected by sleep loss", "the evidence that prolonged wakefulness produces severe cognitive impairment comparable to intoxication"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"striking\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["recently discovered and not yet widely accepted", "surprising and worthy of strong attention", "difficult to measure accurately using standard methods", "deeply troubling and likely to cause long-term harm"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"ubiquitous\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["present or found almost everywhere", "harmful to human health when used in large quantities", "rapidly changing and difficult to keep up with", "designed specifically for use during nighttime hours"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about why changing sleep behavior in society is so difficult?", options: ["Most people are aware that they sleep too little but find it impossible to change their schedules.", "Governments have refused to fund research into sleep because it is not considered a serious health issue.", "Sleep deprivation does not produce visible signs, so people often fail to recognize how seriously it impairs them.", "The improvements from better sleep are too gradual to motivate people to change their behavior."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Growing Threat to Leopards Around the World", paragraphs: ["Of all the large wild cats, the leopard is perhaps the most adaptable. Capable of thriving across an extraordinary range of habitats — from the dense rainforests of central Africa and the arid scrublands of the Arabian Peninsula to the snow-dusted mountain slopes of Central Asia and the agricultural fringes of South Asia — leopards have historically demonstrated a flexibility that allowed them to survive in landscapes where lions, tigers, and cheetahs could not. Yet this celebrated resilience has masked a steady and alarming decline. Across most of their range, leopard populations are shrinking, and in several regions, the species has already vanished from territories it occupied just two generations ago.", "Habitat loss is the most pervasive driver of leopard decline. As human populations expand and agricultural land extends into previously wild areas, the contiguous blocks of territory that leopards need to hunt, reproduce, and maintain viable genetic diversity are being fragmented into increasingly isolated patches. In sub-Saharan Africa, large-scale conversion of savanna and woodland to farmland has reduced leopard range by an estimated 37 percent over the past century. In South and Southeast Asia, deforestation for palm oil, rubber, and timber has eliminated substantial tracts of forest habitat. [1] Fragmentation not only reduces the total area available to leopards but also restricts the movement of individuals between populations, increasing the risk of inbreeding and reducing the population's capacity to recover from local disturbances.", "Prey depletion compounds the problem significantly. Leopards are opportunistic hunters capable of taking a wide variety of prey species, from small rodents and baboons to medium-sized ungulates such as impalas, roe deer, and gazelles. However, in many regions, the animals that leopards depend upon have themselves been reduced by illegal poaching, habitat loss, and competition with domestic livestock. [2] When wild prey becomes scarce, leopards frequently shift to hunting livestock — a behavioral adaptation that brings them into direct and often fatal conflict with farmers and herders who depend on their animals for their livelihoods. Retaliatory killing of leopards by local communities constitutes one of the most immediate threats to the species in both Africa and Asia.", "The illegal wildlife trade adds a further layer of pressure. Leopard skins are traded for use in traditional ceremonial dress in parts of southern Africa, while leopard bones have historically been used as a substitute for tiger bones in some traditional medicine markets across Asia. The CITES appendix listing of leopards as a protected species has reduced — but not eliminated — commercial trade, and enforcement in many range states remains inadequate due to limited resources, corruption, and the vast geographic areas that must be monitored. [3] In some countries, trophy hunting of leopards has also continued under quota systems that conservationists argue are insufficiently regulated and based on outdated population estimates.", "The situation is not uniformly dire. [4] In parts of southern Africa, particularly in Botswana, Zimbabwe, and South Africa, leopard populations have shown resilience where large protected areas, anti-poaching enforcement, and community-based conservation programs have been maintained consistently. In Sri Lanka, the leopard has flourished as the island's apex predator in the absence of tigers, and population density in Yala National Park is considered among the highest of any leopard population worldwide. These pockets of relative stability offer evidence that targeted conservation investment can reverse local decline — but they also highlight, by contrast, the extent of the losses occurring elsewhere.", "Human-leopard conflict remains one of the most complex challenges facing conservation practitioners. Unlike many endangered species that occupy remote wilderness areas, leopards regularly enter villages, farmland, and even the outskirts of large cities in search of food. Attacks on people, though statistically rare compared to livestock predation, generate intense fear and disproportionate media attention that can make community support for leopard conservation politically difficult to sustain. Programs that compensate farmers for livestock losses, deploy livestock protection measures such as predator-proof enclosures, and engage local communities as active stakeholders in conservation decision-making have shown the most promising results in reducing conflict without resorting to lethal control.", "The trajectory of the leopard's future depends less on any single policy or protected area than on whether conservation systems can simultaneously address the multiple, interacting pressures — habitat fragmentation, prey loss, illegal trade, and human conflict — that no individual intervention can fully resolve in isolation. The leopard's extraordinary adaptability has bought time, but the pace of habitat destruction and human encroachment in many range states suggests that without a significant scaling-up of coordinated international effort, that time is running shorter than the species' current distribution might suggest."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main argument the passage makes about leopards?", options: ["Conservation efforts in southern Africa have proven sufficient to stabilize global leopard populations.", "The illegal wildlife trade is the single most destructive force driving leopard populations toward extinction.", "Leopards are less endangered than other big cats because of their superior adaptability.", "Despite their remarkable ability to survive in diverse environments, leopards face multiple serious threats that are causing significant population decline."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, by how much has leopard range declined in sub-Saharan Africa over the past century?", options: ["By an estimated 37 percent", "By more than half of the area leopards previously occupied", "By less than 20 percent, though the rate is accelerating", "By approximately a quarter of its historical extent"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what triggers leopards to begin hunting livestock instead of wild animals?", options: ["Leopards are attracted to the scent of domestic animals, which they find easier to locate than wild prey.", "When wild prey populations fall due to poaching and habitat loss, leopards turn to livestock as an alternative food source.", "Young leopards that have not yet developed full hunting skills prefer slower-moving domestic animals.", "Leopards target livestock primarily during drought seasons when wild animals migrate to wetter areas."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what role do leopard bones play in the illegal wildlife trade?", options: ["They are carved into decorative objects sold to collectors in Europe and North America.", "They are ground into powder and used as a fertilizer in parts of southern Africa.", "They are processed into supplements believed to improve athletic performance in East Asia.", "They are used as a replacement for tiger bones in certain traditional medicine markets in Asia."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is the leopard population in Sri Lanka considered particularly notable?", options: ["Sri Lanka has enacted some of the strictest anti-poaching laws in Asia, protecting the species effectively.", "International conservation funding in Sri Lanka has allowed the government to eliminate all forms of human-leopard conflict.", "The leopard has thrived as the island's top predator without competition from tigers, with Yala National Park having one of the world's densest populations.", "Sri Lanka's leopards have developed unique hunting behaviors not observed in any other leopard population worldwide."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an approach used to reduce human-leopard conflict?", options: ["Involving local communities in conservation planning and decision-making", "Relocating leopards from areas of high conflict to uninhabited wilderness reserves", "Building enclosures that keep livestock safe from predators", "Providing financial compensation to farmers who lose animals to leopard attacks"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 1 refers to _______.", options: ["the two generations of researchers who have studied leopard population decline", "leopards, whose flexibility allowed survival in places other big cats could not manage", "the diverse landscapes across Africa and Asia that leopards inhabit", "lions, tigers, and cheetahs, which also occupy similar habitats"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"resilience\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a preference for living near human settlements rather than deep wilderness", "physical strength that makes the leopard a dominant predator in its ecosystem", "a tendency to expand territorial range when food sources become limited", "the ability to withstand difficult conditions and continue to survive"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"pervasive\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["difficult to reverse once it has begun to take effect", "sudden and unexpected in its appearance", "widespread and present throughout a large area or system", "concentrated in a small number of particularly vulnerable regions"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'In West Africa, the species has effectively disappeared from several countries where it was once widespread, with remaining populations reduced to small, isolated fragments.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Social Classes and Economic Mobility in the United States", paragraphs: ["Few national narratives have proven as resilient — or as contested — as the American Dream: the conviction that individual effort, applied with sufficient determination in a society of open opportunity, can carry a person from the most modest origins to positions of wealth and influence. This narrative has functioned not merely as a cultural aspiration but as a foundational legitimating claim for the American social order, providing ideological justification for economic inequality on the grounds that such inequality is, in principle, traversable by anyone with the drive to ascend. [1] Yet the empirical evidence accumulated over the past four decades tells a more complicated and considerably less flattering story about the actual fluidity of American class boundaries.", "Social scientists distinguish between absolute mobility — the degree to which individuals end up better off than their parents in absolute material terms — and relative mobility, which measures whether a person's position in the income distribution changes relative to others born into similar circumstances. By the first measure, the United States performed well through much of the twentieth century: rising real wages, expanding homeownership, and broadly accessible higher education allowed large numbers of Americans to surpass their parents' standard of living. [2] By the second measure, however, the picture is considerably less favorable. Research by economists Raj Chetty and colleagues, drawing on anonymized tax records covering tens of millions of Americans, has found that a child born in the bottom income quintile in the United States has only an 8 percent chance of reaching the top quintile as an adult — a figure markedly lower than comparable rates in Canada, Denmark, Germany, and Australia.", "The determinants of economic mobility are numerous and mutually reinforcing in ways that make individual effort, while not irrelevant, an insufficient explanation for outcomes. Educational attainment remains the most robust single predictor of adult earnings, yet access to high-quality education in the United States is itself heavily stratified by parental income and residential geography. Public school funding mechanisms tied to local property tax revenues mean that children growing up in lower-income neighborhoods attend schools that are systematically under-resourced compared with those in affluent districts — a structural arrangement that reproduces educational disadvantage across generations with considerable reliability. The proliferation of elite private universities whose admissions processes favor applicants from wealthy families — through legacy preferences, donor admissions, and the outsized advantages conferred by expensive private schooling — compounds this stratification at the upper end of the educational hierarchy.", "[3] Social capital — the networks of relationships, referrals, and informal knowledge that facilitate access to employment, mentorship, and opportunity — is distributed no less unequally than financial capital, and arguably exerts an influence on life outcomes that is more difficult to overcome through individual effort alone. Research in organizational sociology has documented that a substantial proportion of high-paying jobs are filled through personal connections rather than open competitive recruitment, systematically disadvantaging candidates from lower-income backgrounds who are less likely to possess the relevant networks. [4] The concentration of economic activity in a small number of metropolitan areas has further reinforced these dynamics, as the geographic clustering of opportunity creates barriers of physical and financial access that disproportionately affect those with fewer resources to relocate.", "Race intersects with class in ways that render economic mobility even more unequal in practice than aggregate statistics suggest. Black and Hispanic Americans face documented disparities in access to credit, housing, healthcare, and legal representation that cannot be fully explained by income differences alone, reflecting the compounding effects of historical discrimination and ongoing structural disadvantage. Studies examining equivalent job applications — identical in qualifications but varying in names associated with different racial identities — have consistently found that applicants with names perceived as white receive significantly more interview invitations than those with names perceived as Black, even when all other variables are controlled. These findings suggest that the barriers to economic ascent operate not only through structural resource differentials but also through discrimination that persists independently of formal legal prohibitions.", "Defenders of the mobility narrative frequently invoke the existence of exceptional individual success stories — first-generation immigrants who have built substantial businesses, or individuals from impoverished backgrounds who have risen to professional prominence — as evidence that the system is, at least in principle, open to all. Critics counter that the existence of outliers does not constitute evidence of typical outcomes: that a lottery occasionally produces winners does not demonstrate that it is a rational or equitable mechanism for distributing resources. The more pertinent empirical question is not whether upward mobility is possible but how probable it is, and for whom, under current structural conditions.", "The debate over economic mobility is ultimately inseparable from broader questions about what kind of society the United States aspires to be. Whether the persistence of entrenched class boundaries is understood as a temporary market imperfection to be corrected through targeted policy — expanded access to education, healthcare, and affordable housing — or as a structural feature of a system that requires more fundamental redistribution is a question on which the country remains deeply, and perhaps irreducibly, divided. What is harder to dispute, in light of the available evidence, is that the gap between the promise of the American Dream and the statistical reality of economic mobility has widened considerably over the course of the past half-century."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central claim of the passage?", options: ["Economic mobility in the United States is primarily blocked by racial discrimination rather than by structural economic factors.", "Educational reform is the only policy intervention capable of meaningfully improving economic mobility rates in the United States.", "The American Dream is a deliberate myth created by wealthy elites to prevent working-class political mobilization.", "While the ideal of open economic opportunity remains culturally powerful, empirical evidence reveals that actual upward mobility in the United States is considerably more restricted than the national narrative implies."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the American Dream as discussed in the passage?", options: ["Strongly supportive, arguing that the evidence of individual success stories validates the broader narrative", "Indifferent, treating the concept as historically irrelevant to contemporary policy debates", "Openly hostile, dismissing the American Dream as a harmful ideology that should be abandoned entirely", "Analytically skeptical, acknowledging the narrative's cultural power while questioning its alignment with measurable social reality"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does Raj Chetty's research reveal about upward mobility in the United States?", options: ["Relative mobility in the United States has improved significantly since the 1980s due to expanding access to higher education.", "The probability of upward mobility in the United States is highest among children raised in large metropolitan areas.", "Only 8 percent of Americans born into the bottom income quintile will reach the top quintile, a rate lower than in several other developed countries.", "American children born into the lowest income group have roughly the same chance of reaching the top as children in comparable wealthy nations."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the public school funding system in the United States contribute to educational inequality?", options: ["Federal funding formulas deliberately allocate more resources to schools in wealthy areas to reward high academic performance.", "Because school budgets rely on local property taxes, schools in poorer neighborhoods receive systematically less funding than those in wealthier areas.", "Public universities charge significantly higher tuition to students from lower-income zip codes to cross-subsidize scholarships.", "The federal government has reduced investment in public education over the past decade in favor of private school voucher programs."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does the unequal distribution of social capital disadvantage lower-income job seekers?", options: ["Many well-paying positions are filled through personal networks rather than open recruitment, putting those without relevant connections at a structural disadvantage.", "Lower-income applicants tend to have weaker academic qualifications, which social capital cannot compensate for.", "Lower-income individuals typically lack the communication skills needed to convert professional connections into job opportunities.", "Social capital primarily benefits applicants seeking government employment, which represents a declining share of well-paid jobs."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did studies using equivalent job applications with racially identifiable names find?", options: ["Employers in lower-wage sectors showed more racial bias than those hiring for professional roles.", "Discrimination was only detectable in industries where hiring managers had direct contact with applicants during the process.", "The racial gap in interview invitations disappeared entirely when applicants had degrees from highly ranked universities.", "Applicants with names associated with white identities received meaningfully more responses than equally qualified applicants with names associated with Black identities."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT presented as an argument made by defenders of the American mobility narrative?", options: ["The experience of first-generation immigrants building successful businesses supports the idea of opportunity.", "Statistical data on mobility rates is unreliable because it fails to account for individual effort and determination.", "Exceptional individuals from poor backgrounds who achieve professional success demonstrate that the system can be open to all.", "Individual success stories show that upward movement is at least possible within the existing social order."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the public school funding mechanisms used across different states in the United States", "schools located in wealthy districts that receive comparatively greater funding and resources", "the local property tax revenues that determine how much funding a school receives", "children growing up in lower-income neighborhoods who attend under-resourced schools"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"stratified\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["made available to a wider population through the expansion of digital resources", "made deliberately unfair through the actions of self-interested political actors", "divided into distinct layers or levels based on socioeconomic factors", "reduced in quality as a result of insufficient government investment over time"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This ideological function has made the mythology of self-made success particularly resistant to revision, even in the face of contradictory data.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-092", num: 92, title: "Đề 092", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Body Language and Communication Across Cultures", paragraphs: ["When people think about communication, they usually think about the words they use. However, a large part of how we communicate with others does not involve words at all. Body language — the way we use our face, hands, posture, and personal space to send messages — plays a very important role in everyday interaction. Experts believe that in many conversations, more than half of the meaning we convey comes from non-verbal signals rather than from spoken language. Understanding body language can help people communicate more clearly and avoid misunderstandings, especially when they are speaking with someone from a different cultural background.", "One of the most common forms of body language is eye contact. In many Western countries, such as the United States and Australia, making direct eye contact during a conversation is seen as a sign of confidence, honesty, and respect. People who avoid looking at the person they are speaking to may be perceived as shy, untrustworthy, or disinterested. In contrast, in some East Asian and Southeast Asian cultures, prolonged eye contact with someone older or in a position of authority can be considered rude or disrespectful . In these cultures, looking slightly downward when speaking to an elder is a way of showing politeness and acknowledgement of the other person's status.", "Gestures are another area where cultural differences can lead to confusion. The thumbs-up sign, for example, is understood in most Western countries as a positive signal meaning \"good\" or \"I agree.\" However, in parts of the Middle East and West Africa, the same gesture carries a very different, and highly offensive, meaning. Similarly, the \"OK\" sign made by connecting the thumb and index finger in a circle is a friendly gesture in countries like the United States and the United Kingdom, but is considered rude in Brazil and some parts of southern Europe. Even nodding the head — which most people assume to mean \"yes\" everywhere — is used to mean \"no\" in Bulgaria and parts of Greece.", "Personal space is also an important aspect of non-verbal communication, and preferences for how much space people feel comfortable with during a conversation vary significantly across cultures. In Latin American and Middle Eastern cultures, people often stand quite close to one another when talking, and this closeness is a sign of warmth and friendliness. In Northern European countries such as Finland, Sweden, and Denmark, however, people generally prefer a larger personal distance, and standing too close to someone during a conversation may make them feel uncomfortable or even threatened. When people from these very different cultural backgrounds meet and interact, the difference in spatial expectations can cause one person to keep moving closer while the other keeps stepping away — a kind of accidental dance that neither participant fully understands.", "Facial expressions are often considered more universal than other forms of body language. Research by the psychologist Paul Ekman in the 1960s and 1970s suggested that six basic emotions — happiness, sadness, anger, surprise, fear, and disgust — are expressed through the same facial movements across all human cultures. This research was widely cited as evidence that at least some forms of body language are innate rather than learned. More recent studies, however, have raised questions about how universal these expressions truly are, suggesting that the way people display and interpret facial expressions may be influenced to a greater degree by cultural norms than Ekman's original research implied.", "Learning to read body language accurately in cross-cultural situations is a skill that takes both knowledge and practice. Travellers, business professionals, diplomats, and teachers who regularly interact with people from different backgrounds can benefit greatly from developing this awareness. Simple mistakes — such as using a gesture that is perfectly acceptable at home but offensive elsewhere — can damage relationships and create unnecessary tension. Paying close attention to how people around you are using their bodies, and being willing to adapt your own behaviour when needed, is one of the most effective ways to build understanding and trust across cultural differences.", "In a world where people of different cultural backgrounds interact more frequently than ever before, body language awareness has become an increasingly valuable skill. While no one can be expected to memorise every cultural rule about gestures, eye contact, and personal space, developing a general sensitivity to the fact that non-verbal communication is not universal is a first and important step toward more respectful and effective cross-cultural communication."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Facial expressions are the only form of body language that is the same in all cultures around the world", "Western cultures have the most developed systems of non-verbal communication", "Spoken language is always more important than body language in everyday communication", "Body language carries different meanings across cultures, and understanding these differences is essential for effective communication"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, in countries like the United States and Australia, a person who avoids eye contact during a conversation may be seen as ______.", options: ["shy, untrustworthy, or not interested in the conversation", "thoughtful and careful before giving a response", "calm, relaxed, and at ease in social situations", "polite and respectful toward the other person"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, in parts of the Middle East and West Africa, the thumbs-up gesture is ______.", options: ["a sign of agreement and approval, similar to its meaning in Western countries", "a signal used only in formal business settings to indicate a successful deal", "an offensive gesture that carries a very different meaning from its Western interpretation", "a common greeting used when meeting someone for the first time"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following countries uses a head nod to mean \"no\" rather than \"yes\"?", options: ["Brazil", "Bulgaria", "Finland", "Australia"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The passage describes a situation in which two people from very different cultural backgrounds keep moving toward and away from each other during a conversation. This happens because ______.", options: ["one person is trying to intimidate the other through aggressive body language", "the two people have different expectations about how much personal space is appropriate", "both people are unfamiliar with the language being spoken and are trying to hear more clearly", "one person is following rules of formal etiquette while the other is behaving casually"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT listed as one of the six basic emotions identified in Paul Ekman's research?", options: ["Jealousy", "Disgust", "Surprise", "Fear"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"disrespectful\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["showing a lack of politeness or regard for another person", "being overly confident in a social situation", "behaving in an unusual or unexpected way", "appearing nervous or uncomfortable during a conversation"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"universal\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["related to emotions rather than physical actions", "ancient and long-established", "applying equally to all people and places", "based on scientific research and experiments"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 4 (\"may make them feel uncomfortable or even threatened\") refers to _______.", options: ["business professionals and diplomats who travel frequently", "people from Northern European countries such as Finland, Sweden, and Denmark", "people who prefer standing very close to others during conversations", "people from Latin American and Middle Eastern cultures"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To entertain readers with amusing stories about cross-cultural misunderstandings", "To argue that Western body language norms should be adopted as a global standard", "To persuade readers to study the work of psychologist Paul Ekman in greater depth", "To inform readers about the cultural differences in body language and encourage greater awareness and sensitivity in cross-cultural communication"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "One Man's Mission: David Katz and the Fight Against Plastic Waste", paragraphs: ["Every year, an estimated eight million tonnes of plastic waste enters the world's oceans, threatening marine ecosystems, entering the food chain, and accumulating on coastlines that were once celebrated for their natural beauty. For most people, this figure is a source of dismay but not necessarily a prompt for action. For David Katz, a Canadian social entrepreneur, it became the starting point for one of the most innovative and widely discussed environmental initiatives of the past decade: the Plastic Bank.", "Katz founded the Plastic Bank in 2013 with a deceptively simple premise — that plastic pollution could be addressed not by treating it primarily as an environmental problem, but by reframing it as an economic opportunity. The core idea was to establish a system in which people living in coastal communities in developing countries could collect plastic waste from beaches, rivers, and streets, and exchange it for goods, services, or digital currency at designated collection centres. The collected plastic would then be processed and sold to companies committed to incorporating recycled materials into their products — labelled \"Social Plastic\" to signal that the material had been gathered in a way that simultaneously benefited both the environment and the people who collected it.", "The initiative was launched first in Haiti, one of the countries most severely affected by both extreme poverty and plastic pollution. Haiti presented a particularly difficult operating environment: limited infrastructure, political instability, and a population in which a significant proportion of people lacked access to basic banking services. Katz and his team responded to these challenges by partnering with a financial technology company to provide collectors with a digital account accessible via mobile phone, allowing them to store and transfer their earnings without needing a traditional bank account. This approach not only made the system more accessible to the very poorest participants but also gave collectors a degree of financial security and independence that many had not previously experienced.", "By 2020, the Plastic Bank had expanded its operations to include communities in the Philippines, Indonesia, Egypt, and Brazil — countries chosen both for the scale of their plastic pollution problem and for the size of the economically vulnerable coastal populations who could benefit from the programme. In each location, the organisation worked to establish a network of collection branches staffed by local community members, creating employment as well as environmental impact. Collectors could exchange their plastic for a range of items including cooking fuel, school tuition for their children, medical insurance, and mobile phone credit — a deliberate design choice that addressed the most pressing everyday needs of participating families rather than offering only cash payments.", "The Plastic Bank's model has attracted both admiration and critical scrutiny. Supporters argue that it represents a genuinely creative approach to a problem that conventional regulatory and clean-up efforts have largely failed to solve — one that aligns financial incentives with environmental goals in a way that is self-sustaining rather than dependent on charitable donations. Critics, however, have raised concerns about the scale of the impact relative to the enormity of the global plastic problem. Even at its most productive, the amount of plastic collected by the Plastic Bank represents a small fraction of the waste that enters the ocean annually, and some environmental economists argue that market-based solutions of this kind, however innovative, cannot substitute for binding international agreements and systemic reductions in plastic production at the manufacturing level.", "David Katz has engaged openly with these criticisms, acknowledging that the Plastic Bank alone cannot solve the plastic crisis but arguing that it addresses a dimension of the problem that purely regulatory approaches cannot reach — the vast quantities of plastic that are already present in vulnerable coastal environments, far from the reach of any government clean-up programme. He also points to the social impact data the organisation has collected, which suggests that participation in the programme is associated with measurable improvements in food security, school attendance, and household income among collecting families. For Katz, the value of the Plastic Bank lies not only in the tonnes of plastic it removes from the environment but in the proof of concept it provides: that environmental problems and social inequality can, at least in some cases, be addressed simultaneously through a single well-designed intervention.", "Whether the Plastic Bank ultimately achieves the scale necessary to make a meaningful dent in global plastic pollution remains to be seen. What is already clear, however, is that the project has shifted the terms of the conversation about plastic waste in a way that few initiatives have managed — demonstrating that the people most vulnerable to the effects of environmental degradation need not be passive victims of a problem they did not create, but can instead be active agents in its solution."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The global scale of ocean plastic pollution and why governments have failed to address it", "David Katz's Plastic Bank initiative — how it works, where it operates, and the debate surrounding its impact", "How digital banking technology is being used to improve financial access for people in developing nations", "A comparison of different recycling programmes operating in developing countries across Asia and Latin America"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, the core idea behind the Plastic Bank was to ______.", options: ["convince large corporations to stop manufacturing single-use plastic products", "establish a global network of beaches monitored and cleaned by trained environmental volunteers", "treat plastic pollution as an economic opportunity by allowing people to exchange collected plastic for goods or currency", "pressure governments in developing countries to pass stricter anti-pollution laws"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, the Plastic Bank addressed the lack of traditional banking access in Haiti by ______.", options: ["working with the Haitian government to establish a national recycling subsidy programme", "providing collectors with prepaid debit cards that could be used at local markets", "partnering with a financial technology company to give collectors a digital account accessible via mobile phone", "opening a network of physical bank branches specifically for plastic collectors"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, the range of items offered to plastic collectors in exchange for their plastic was designed to ______.", options: ["allow the organisation to avoid paying cash, which was difficult to distribute in remote areas", "meet the most urgent practical needs of participating families in their daily lives", "promote environmentally friendly products and services within local communities", "encourage collectors to gather as much plastic as possible by offering luxury rewards"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, critics of the Plastic Bank have argued that ______.", options: ["the programme exploits poor communities by paying them unfairly for their labour", "market-based solutions cannot replace the need for binding international agreements and reductions in plastic production", "the organisation has expanded too quickly to maintain the quality of its operations in each country", "the digital payment system used by the programme is too unreliable for use in developing countries"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an item that plastic collectors can receive in exchange for their collected plastic?", options: ["Cooking fuel", "School tuition for their children", "Clean drinking water", "Mobile phone credit"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"innovative\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["introducing new and creative methods that have not been used before", "extremely expensive and requiring significant investment", "internationally recognised and legally protected", "well-established and proven over many years of use"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"addressed\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["described or explained to an audience", "dealt with or responded to a specific need or problem", "formally complained about to an authority", "delivered directly to a specific location"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 2 (\"exchange it for goods, services, or digital currency\") refers to _______.", options: ["the financial benefit received by participating companies", "plastic waste collected from beaches, rivers, and streets", "digital currency", "the collection centre where exchanges take place"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the author's view of the Plastic Bank?", options: ["The author is skeptical that the Plastic Bank will ever attract enough participants to make a difference", "The author believes the Plastic Bank has already solved the global plastic pollution crisis", "The author regards the Plastic Bank as significant primarily because it has changed how people think about the roles of vulnerable communities in environmental solutions", "The author argues that the Plastic Bank should focus exclusively on social benefits rather than environmental ones"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Exercise Timing and Cognitive Performance: The Science Behind Afternoon Workouts", paragraphs: ["The question of when to exercise has historically received far less scientific attention than the question of how much exercise people should do. In recent years, however, a growing body of research has begun to suggest that the timing of physical activity may have a more significant influence on cognitive performance — including concentration, memory consolidation, and executive function — than was previously appreciated. Of particular interest to researchers is the relationship between afternoon exercise and the mental demands of work and study that typically dominate the second half of the working day.", "Human physiology follows a roughly twenty-four-hour internal clock known as the circadian rhythm, which regulates a wide range of biological processes including body temperature, hormone secretion, and alertness. Core body temperature, for example, typically reaches its daily peak in the mid-to-late afternoon — between approximately 2 PM and 6 PM in most adults — and this thermal peak is associated with increased muscular efficiency, faster reaction times, and a greater capacity for sustained physical effort. Athletes and sports scientists have long noted that performance on tasks requiring speed, strength, and coordination tends to be measurably superior when training takes place during this window compared to early morning sessions.", "What is perhaps less widely known is the extent to which this afternoon physiological peak also appears to enhance cognitive performance following exercise. A landmark study conducted by researchers at the University of Copenhagen found that a single session of moderate aerobic exercise performed in the mid-afternoon produced significantly greater improvements in working memory and attention scores — measured two hours after the session concluded — compared to equivalent exercise performed in the morning. The researchers attributed this effect partly to elevated levels of brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF), a protein that supports the growth and maintenance of neurons and is released in higher concentrations during afternoon exercise than during morning workouts at equivalent intensities. [1]", "The relationship between exercise and afternoon workload is further complicated by the phenomenon known as the post-lunch dip — a well-documented decline in alertness and cognitive performance that affects most people between roughly 1 PM and 3 PM, regardless of whether they have eaten lunch. [2] This dip is now understood to be a genetically encoded feature of human circadian biology rather than simply the consequence of eating a large meal, and it represents one of the most reliable productivity challenges of the working day. Research has demonstrated that a short bout of moderate exercise — as brief as twenty minutes of brisk walking — can attenuate this dip significantly, restoring alertness to near-morning levels for a period of up to two hours. [3] This finding has practical implications for employers, educators, and individuals seeking to maintain consistent cognitive output across the full span of the working day. [4]", "Not all forms of exercise produce equivalent cognitive benefits in the afternoon context. High-intensity interval training, while highly effective for cardiovascular fitness, has been shown to temporarily impair certain aspects of executive function in the immediate post-exercise period — a phenomenon attributed to the competing demands that intense physical effort places on prefrontal cortex resources. Moderate aerobic activity, by contrast, appears to occupy a cognitive sweet spot: it is sufficiently demanding to trigger the neurochemical cascades associated with improved mood and alertness, without temporarily depleting the attentional resources required for complex cognitive tasks. This distinction matters in practical terms for anyone planning a lunchtime or early-afternoon workout before returning to demanding knowledge work.", "The implications of this research extend beyond individual productivity into the domain of workplace and educational policy. Several Scandinavian countries have begun experimenting with structured physical activity breaks during the school day, positioned deliberately in the early afternoon to coincide with the post-lunch dip. Preliminary data from these programmes suggest that students who participate in a twenty-to-thirty-minute guided exercise session at this time demonstrate measurably better performance on subsequent concentration tasks compared to peers who spend the equivalent period in sedentary rest. Similar experiments in corporate environments have yielded comparable results, with employees who exercised during afternoon breaks reporting higher levels of self-rated cognitive engagement and completing structured cognitive tasks with greater accuracy in the hours following exercise.", "Despite the convergence of evidence in favour of afternoon exercise for cognitive performance, individual variation remains significant. Chronotype — the genetically influenced tendency toward being a morning person or an evening person — can shift the precise timing of physiological peaks by as much as two to three hours in either direction. For confirmed evening types, or \"owls,\" the cognitive benefits of afternoon exercise may be even more pronounced, as their circadian peak tends to occur later than the population average. For morning types, or \"larks,\" the benefits may be slightly less dramatic in the afternoon but are unlikely to be absent entirely. The practical message that emerges from this body of research is not that everyone should abandon morning exercise in favour of afternoon sessions, but that individuals and institutions alike have good reason to take seriously the timing of physical activity as a variable worth deliberately optimising."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main argument of the passage?", options: ["The timing of exercise — particularly in the afternoon — has meaningful effects on cognitive performance that individuals and institutions should take seriously", "High-intensity exercise is more beneficial than moderate aerobic activity regardless of what time of day it is performed", "People should replace morning exercise routines with afternoon sessions for better physical fitness", "Circadian rhythms make it impossible for morning types to benefit from afternoon physical activity"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, the daily peak in core body temperature in most adults occurs ______.", options: ["between approximately 12 PM and 2 PM", "between approximately 9 AM and 12 PM", "between approximately 6 PM and 9 PM", "between approximately 2 PM and 6 PM"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, the University of Copenhagen study found that afternoon exercise produced greater improvements in working memory and attention than morning exercise partly because ______.", options: ["morning exercise was found to actively impair working memory in the hours following the session", "higher concentrations of BDNF — a protein supporting neuron growth — are released during afternoon exercise", "the afternoon environment was quieter and less distracting for the participants", "participants in the afternoon group exercised for longer periods than those in the morning group"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, the post-lunch dip is best understood as ______.", options: ["a side effect of caffeine consumption that disrupts afternoon alertness patterns", "a temporary energy loss caused exclusively by eating a large meal at lunchtime", "an unavoidable productivity loss that cannot be reduced through any form of physical intervention", "a genetically encoded feature of circadian biology that occurs regardless of food intake"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is high-intensity interval training considered less suitable than moderate aerobic exercise before afternoon knowledge work?", options: ["It produces lower levels of BDNF compared to moderate aerobic exercise at any time of day", "It takes significantly longer to complete and therefore reduces the time available for work", "It can temporarily impair executive function by placing competing demands on prefrontal cortex resources", "It causes muscle fatigue that makes it physically uncomfortable to sit at a desk afterwards"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT listed as a benefit associated with the afternoon physiological peak?", options: ["Greater capacity for sustained physical effort", "Increased muscular efficiency", "Reduced need for sleep and recovery", "Faster reaction times"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"attenuate\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["eliminate completely and permanently", "delay the onset of by several hours", "measure and record with scientific precision", "reduce in strength or intensity"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"dominate\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["interfere with and disrupt", "take up or occupy most of", "occur occasionally throughout", "begin slowly and build gradually during"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The phrase \"this finding\" in paragraph 4 (\"This finding has practical implications for employers, educators, and individuals\") refers to the discovery that _______.", options: ["BDNF is released in higher concentrations during afternoon exercise than morning exercise", "around twenty minutes of moderate exercise can restore afternoon alertness for up to two hours", "the post-lunch dip is a genetically encoded feature of human circadian biology", "most people experience a decline in alertness between 1 PM and 3 PM regardless of their lunch"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This neurochemical advantage may help explain why many knowledge workers anecdotally report feeling sharper and more focused on afternoons when they have exercised at midday.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Climate Change and the Limits of the Global Response", paragraphs: ["Few challenges in modern history have demanded the level of coordinated international action that climate change requires, and fewer still have exposed as starkly the gap between the scale of a problem and the collective willingness to address it. Since the adoption of the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change in 1992, the international community has constructed an increasingly elaborate architecture of agreements, commitments, and monitoring mechanisms — from the Kyoto Protocol of 1997 to the landmark Paris Agreement of 2015 — each presented at the time of its signing as a decisive turning point. [1] And yet global greenhouse gas emissions, after thirty years of diplomacy, continue to rise. The gap between the rhetoric of climate ambition and the reality of national policy implementation is not merely a communications problem; it reflects deep structural tensions that no single agreement, however well-designed, has yet managed to resolve.", "The Paris Agreement represented a genuine departure from the architecture of its predecessors. Rather than imposing legally binding emissions targets from above, it invited each signatory nation to submit its own Nationally Determined Contribution — a self-defined pledge outlining the emissions reductions that country intended to achieve by 2030. [2] The logic behind this voluntary approach was pragmatic: the failure of the Kyoto Protocol had demonstrated that binding targets without meaningful enforcement mechanisms invited non-compliance and withdrawal, as the United States' rejection of the protocol had illustrated with damaging visibility. By allowing countries to set their own ambition levels, the Paris framework sought to maximise participation at the cost of guaranteed sufficiency — a trade-off that its architects openly acknowledged and that critics have never stopped debating.", "The inadequacy of current commitments is not a matter of serious scientific dispute. The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change has repeatedly concluded that the aggregate effect of all nationally determined contributions submitted under the Paris Agreement — even if fully implemented — would result in warming of approximately 2.5 to 3 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels by the end of this century, well above the 1.5-degree threshold that climate scientists identify as the boundary beyond which the risk of catastrophic and potentially irreversible changes to Earth's major systems increases exponentially . The emissions reductions required to remain within that threshold would necessitate changes to energy, transport, agriculture, and land use of a speed and scale that no democratic government has yet demonstrated the political capacity to deliver.", "The political economy of climate action is, in many respects, the central obstacle. The costs of decarbonisation — restructuring energy infrastructure, retiring fossil fuel industries, retraining affected workers, and managing the distributional consequences of higher energy prices — fall with concentrated and immediate force on specific industries, regions, and electoral constituencies. The benefits, by contrast, are diffuse, global, and long-term — accruing disproportionately to future generations and to populations in the Global South who bear the heaviest burden of climate impacts despite having contributed least to the accumulation of atmospheric carbon. This asymmetry between concentrated near-term costs and dispersed long-term benefits creates a structural incentive for democratic governments to under-deliver on climate commitments, regardless of the sincerity of the intentions expressed at the negotiating table.", "The role of fossil fuel interests in shaping — and often distorting — the political landscape of climate policy deserves particular scrutiny. Decades of strategic lobbying, the funding of think tanks that cast doubt on mainstream climate science, and the cultivation of political alliances with representatives of fossil-fuel-dependent regions have demonstrably delayed and weakened climate legislation in several major emitting economies, most notably the United States, Australia, and Brazil. Internal documents released through litigation have revealed that some major oil companies had detailed knowledge of the likely trajectory of climate change as early as the 1970s and chose to fund doubt-manufacturing campaigns rather than disclose this knowledge or pivot their business models — a revelation that has fundamentally altered the moral framing of the climate debate.", "Technological optimism offers one source of qualified hope amid this otherwise sombre picture. The cost of solar photovoltaic electricity generation has fallen by more than 90 percent since 2010, and the cost of lithium-ion battery storage has declined at a comparable rate, making the clean energy transition economically viable in ways that were genuinely unforeseeable a decade ago. Electric vehicle adoption is accelerating across major markets. Carbon capture and storage technology, while still expensive and unproven at the scale required, continues to attract significant research investment. Some analysts argue that the combined effect of these technological trajectories — interacting with strengthening policy frameworks and shifting consumer preferences — may produce a faster-than-expected decarbonisation of several key economic sectors in the 2030s. Whether this optimism is vindicated will depend largely on the degree to which governments are willing to translate technological possibility into binding regulatory requirements rather than leaving the pace of transition to the discretion of markets alone.", "What the climate crisis ultimately exposes is a profound mismatch between the timescales of democratic politics — where electoral cycles of four to five years create incentives for short-term thinking — and the timescales of atmospheric physics, where the consequences of today's emissions decisions will not be fully felt for decades. Closing this temporal gap is perhaps the deepest governance challenge of the era, and it is one that no existing international institution is currently equipped to resolve. The question is not whether the science is clear — it is — but whether the political and economic structures through which human societies make collective decisions are adequate to the demands of a challenge that operates on a timescale that those structures were never designed to accommodate."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Despite three decades of international climate diplomacy, deep structural tensions between short-term political incentives and long-term climate imperatives continue to prevent adequate global action", "Fossil fuel companies bear sole responsibility for the failure of the international community to address climate change effectively", "The Paris Agreement is fundamentally flawed and should be replaced by a legally binding global treaty with enforceable penalties", "Technological progress in renewable energy has made climate change a solvable problem that governments are failing to take advantage of"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, the Paris Agreement's voluntary approach to emissions targets was adopted primarily because ______.", options: ["scientific uncertainty at the time made it impossible to agree on specific numerical targets", "developing countries refused to participate in any framework that imposed mandatory emissions reductions", "negotiators believed that voluntary pledges would produce more ambitious commitments than legally binding targets", "the failure of the Kyoto Protocol had shown that binding targets without enforcement mechanisms encouraged non-compliance and withdrawal"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The passage implies that the current nationally determined contributions submitted under the Paris Agreement are insufficient because ______.", options: ["even full implementation of all existing pledges would result in warming well above the 1.5-degree safety threshold", "fewer than half of the world's nations have submitted any pledges at all", "the pledges focus exclusively on carbon dioxide and ignore other greenhouse gases entirely", "most countries have already confirmed they will not meet the targets they have submitted"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do democratic governments tend to under-deliver on climate commitments, even when they are sincerely intended?", options: ["International agreements prevent individual governments from implementing unilateral climate policies", "The immediate costs of decarbonisation fall on specific groups, while the long-term benefits are dispersed and accrue largely to future generations", "Climate change is a technically complex issue that most politicians lack the expertise to understand", "Voters in most democracies consistently rank climate change as a low priority compared to economic issues"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes the author's view of technological developments in clean energy?", options: ["They offer qualified grounds for optimism, but their impact depends on governments translating technological possibility into binding regulation", "They have been consistently overstated by analysts and are unlikely to accelerate decarbonisation in any meaningful timeframe", "They are progressing too slowly to make any difference to global emissions before the end of the century", "They represent a complete solution to the climate crisis that makes policy intervention unnecessary"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a tactic used by fossil fuel interests to influence climate policy?", options: ["Cultivating political alliances with representatives of fossil-fuel-dependent regions", "Strategic lobbying of government officials", "Funding think tanks that questioned mainstream climate science", "Filing legal challenges against climate legislation in national courts"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"exponentially\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["gradually and in a predictable, linear pattern", "suddenly and without any prior warning", "in a way that is difficult to observe without scientific instruments", "at a rate that accelerates rapidly, reaching very large magnitudes"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"cast doubt\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["make people uncertain or suspicious about the validity of something", "conduct rigorous independent research into", "openly acknowledge the limitations of existing scientific models", "formally disprove through peer-reviewed scientific study"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following best describes the author's overall attitude toward the international response to climate change?", options: ["Indifferent — the author presents multiple perspectives without indicating any personal position on the adequacy of current efforts", "Critically analytical — the author acknowledges diplomatic progress and technological hope while systematically identifying the structural barriers that continue to prevent adequate action", "Optimistic — the author believes that recent technological progress makes a successful global response increasingly likely", "Despairing — the author concludes that climate change is an unsolvable problem that human institutions are inherently incapable of addressing"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Each of these milestones generated considerable political momentum and media attention, yet none produced the systemic transformation in global energy and land use that scientists had identified as necessary.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "At the beginning of the passage", "At the end of the passage"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
];
